High-Risk Atherosclerotic Complications
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Vasculogenic erectile dysfunction, Intermittent claudication, Diminished peripheral pulses, High 10-year absolute cardiovascular risk

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-017
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of severe, unrelenting resting calf pain and acute chest tightness, necessitating immediate evaluation for acute limb ischaemia and unstable angina.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage highlighted the progression of chronic claudication into severe, unremitting resting pain in the lower extremity, alongside new-onset exertional chest tightness. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to rule out critical limb ischaemia and acute coronary syndrome. Delaying intervention until the next in-hours period carried an unacceptably high risk of imminent myocardial infarction or irreversible limb necrosis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My leg is cramping so severely even while I am lying down that it feels dead, and my chest feels heavy and tight."
History of Present Illness: Over the past eight months, the patient has noted a progressive onset of severe vasculogenic erectile dysfunction characterized by a consistent inability to achieve a rigid erection. This was followed by intermittent claudication presenting as reproducible calf pain upon exertion, which has now acutely transitioned to severe, unremitting pain at rest over the last four hours. Today, they also experienced exertional angina pectoris for the first time after walking a short distance. They note central abdominal obesity indicative of the metabolic syndrome and a long history of untreated hypertension.
Functional Status: Immobile due to severe ischemic rest pain in the lower extremity.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of cardiovascular collapse; spouse is highly distressed by the patient's acute chest symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/100 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.6C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 112 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, displaying central obesity, visibly anxious, and clutching the left calf while taking shallow breaths.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Diminished distal peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) bilaterally, with the left foot noticeably cooler than the right. A carotid bruit is present on auscultation. A left ventricular heave is palpable, indicating chronic hypertensive heart disease. Ophthalmic: Retinal arteriolar narrowing and silver-wiring visible on direct fundoscopy. Dermatological: Xanthelasmas noted on the eyelids due to severe dyslipidaemia. Expected investigation results will show a markedly elevated 10-year absolute cardiovascular risk score calculated utilizing abnormal fasting lipid profiles, elevated HbA1c, and an abnormal resting electrocardiogram.
Environmental Observations: Empty packets of high-sodium, highly processed foods visible; home environment lacks any structure for physical activity.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient has a history of non-compliance with previously prescribed statins and antihypertensives.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: High-Risk Atherosclerotic Complications - Status: Exacerbated (Critical Limb Ischaemia and Unstable Angina)
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the distinct absence of unilateral leg swelling, warmth, or erythema, and the specific finding of diminished arterial pulses.

Psychogenic Erectile Dysfunction - Ruled out/unlikely given the extensive, objective evidence of severe systemic vascular disease, including bruits and claudication.

Musculoskeletal Calf Strain - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain occurring at complete rest, the cool temperature of the foot, and the concurrent presentation of anginal chest pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits end-stage systemic manifestations of untreated metabolic syndrome, presenting acutely with critical limb ischaemia (rest pain, cool extremity, diminished pulses) and unstable angina (new-onset chest tightness). The sudden progression from exertional claudication to severe resting pain indicates critical narrowing or acute plaque rupture in the peripheral vasculature, mirroring the unstable pathology in the coronary arteries. Urgent home assessment was essential to perform a comprehensive cardiovascular examination and immediately initiate antiplatelet therapy. Waiting for routine daytime primary care would inevitably result in permanent ischemic tissue loss in the limb or a fatal acute myocardial infarction.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Aspirin 300mg chewed immediately to provide rapid antiplatelet effect. Administered sublingual Glyceryl Trinitrate 400mcg spray, which partially relieved the chest tightness. Arranged immediate ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent vascular and cardiology assessment.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage severe dyslipidaemia moving forward, the patient is instructed to implement a visual portion control strategy. By using a physically smaller plate for all meals, the patient naturally reduces caloric and saturated fat intake without complex tracking. Seeing a fully loaded, albeit smaller, plate satisfies the psychological need for a full meal, reinforcing this sustainable dietary modification.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Handover provided to paramedics for continuous ECG monitoring during transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None, requires immediate hospital admission.
Follow-up: General practice review is required post-hospital discharge for aggressive secondary prevention and metabolic syndrome management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance transfer is arranged. The patient must take another dose of the GTN spray in 5 minutes if the chest pain returns or intensifies prior to the arrival of the paramedics.

